</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 493)</h2>
<p><b>A look at 0% is exactly no look, and look chips take the click.</b></p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed: turning a look down to 0% still rolled off the whites.</b> A look at reduced strength is baked into a lattice blended toward identity — but a lattice is only identity inside the 0–1 range of its working space, and log material carries plenty of picture above that. Everything over range clamped at the lattice edge: measured, a 2-stop-over value displayed 9% dark and a 4-stop-over value 14% dark, both flattened to the same ceiling — with the look at zero. A layer at 0% is now dropped from the chain entirely, verified pixel-identical to no look at all.</li>
<li><b>Verified both color modes while in there:</b> the same Film Lab look renders the same on a native EXR and on its ACEScct optimized stand-in — channel means agree within 0.2%, with the remaining difference being 4K-vs-HD detail, not color.</li>
<li><b>Fixed: look chips ignoring clicks.</b> Each chip's thumbnail spills invisibly outside its pill, and that spill was a live click region sitting on top of the neighboring chips — so clicks landed on a neighbor's invisible overflow instead of the chip under the cursor. The click target is now exactly the pill.</li>
<li>Worth knowing: at strengths above 0%, a print-film look still compresses values above diffuse white — that is what a display-referred print emulation does. If a look should leave super-whites alone, that is a pipeline-placement change worth discussing, not a slider fix.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
